## How users think about it
- **Monday planning (POC research).** Merchants set direction on Monday and sent it to Originators, then revised mid-week as futures and field intel moved. Jonathan and Reah quotes above; research agent `market_scenario` (from user note `user-note-2026-04-01-weekly-loop-monday-scenario`). That was partly how they worked and partly because the POC made them rebuild each time. The new model doesn't design around Monday. The target is automatic refresh (today it's manual). Source: Brenden, 2026-10-10.
- **Something they publish to people.** The output is direction for Originators, not just a calculation. Research agent `market_scenario` → produces `guidance_package`; Jonathan quote above.
- **One core, two modes.** Some merchants use scenarios to read the market, others to drive execution, targeting and handoff. Research agent `pricing_scenario` (evidence: Ben Schulenberg shadowing, Jonathan Baker connect).
- **Should persist and be quick to change.** Defaults, saved state, fast edits. Jonathan quote; [Merchant persona](../Personas/Merchant.md) ("No multi-session support").
- **Build every window, then roll forward.** Usage data from Sep 1 to Oct 21 shows two patterns:
  - Building every window on day one: Ian Bowles built 9 Albion scenarios on Sep 22, and Artur Bogomolov built Cedar Rapids on Sep 23. Artur archived the Jan–Mar windows on Oct 8.
  - Rolling the nearest window forward: Matt Carmody has made 6 Kansas City S2026X scenarios, archiving the old one each time.

  Only 1 of the 20 archived Merchant scenarios was rebuilt with the same identity. Source: Kernel Usage Metrics v9 report (Activity audit + scenarios API).

